0..74 Comment "// `map`\'s functoriality stated as a law beside the definition it governs:"
75..152 Comment "// mapping a composite is mapping twice (Bird, _An Introduction to the Theory"
153..231 Comment "// of Lists_, 1987, § 2). The list `map` of `recursive-lists.gandr` takes its"
232..308 Comment "// function as a value, and a rule names only its block\'s operations, so the"
309..380 Comment "// law is stated over `map` presented as an operation with its function"
381..455 Comment "// defunctionalised (Reynolds, _Definitional Interpreters for Higher-Order"
456..529 Comment "// Programming Languages_, 1972): a function is a name `app` applies, and"
530..608 Comment "// `compose` builds one from two. The coincidence check certifies the law with"
609..687 Comment "// the definition\'s own rules, and the `normalizes` row fires it. The same law"
688..777 Comment "// written over the `def` is refused in `../../pathological/data/map-law-on-a-def.gandr`."
779..850 Comment "/// Lists with `map` as an operation: `mapNil` and `mapCons` define it,"
851..926 Comment "/// `appCompose` reads a composite, `composeAssoc` orients composition, and"
927..990 Comment "/// `mapMap` is the law, mapping twice fused into mapping once."
991..993 Other "@["
994..1004 Other "normalizes"
1004..1005 Other "("
1005..1006 StringLit "\""
1006..1078 StringLit "map(inc, map(dbl, cons(one, nil))) ~> cons(app(inc, app(dbl, one)), nil)"
1078..1079 StringLit "\""
1079..1080 Other ")"
1081..1082 Other "]"
